From c8b24b67ef5319a4000a840bc13b1e12c16195ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: broken-branch <broken-branch@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 10:10:35 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Config, command line and sensor matching

Parse the configuration into a Config with one SensorSpec per panel
position. A missing key or an unparsable update_interval is an error
at start (exit status 2) instead of a silent fallback to defaults, and
update_interval has a floor of 100 ms. PCI IDs accept 0x, 0X or no
prefix, ignore leading zeros, and must match exactly; the previous
substring comparison accepted "14e" for 14e3. Config::parse is split
from the file read so the parser has tests.

Add --list-sensors, which prints every temperature libsensors can see
with the chip's PCI [vendor:device], and --help. Usage errors go to
stderr with exit status 2. The sensor dump that ran on every start is
gone; start-up now prints the configured specs and the chip and label
each one matched.

Sensor matching: consider only temperature features and their _input
subfeature; match the chip name only (previously a feature label was
also accepted for cpu_device/gpu_device, which the README never
documented); read the PCI ID from the chip's hwmon device link, falling
back to its parent for class devices such as NVMe; take the first
matching chip in libsensors order rather than the last, and when an
unpinned spec matches more than one chip, say which ones and which was
chosen, so the vendor/device ID options are discoverable from the log.
